# Issue Log and Decision Log — Monitoring and Controlling
#
# The issue log is PMBOK Guide 6th ed., 4.3 Direct and Manage Project Work.
# The decision log with its provenance states is not in the standard: it is
# the sponsor's own rule, from 2026-08-27.

screen: issues
title: Issues and Decisions
phase: Monitoring and Controlling
process: 4.3 Direct and Manage Project Work
order: 85
requires: charter
teaches: |
  How to keep problems and uncertainties apart, and why a decision record is
  only worth having if every entry shows where it came from. By the end you
  should be able to explain why an issue with no owner and no date is not being
  managed.
before: |
  Nothing. Issues and decisions accumulate from day one, so this screen is
  worth keeping open from the start rather than filling in at the end.
after: |
  An honest closeout. Lessons learned written from a decision log are
  specific; lessons written from memory are generous to the person writing
  them.
intro: |
  An issue is something that has already happened and needs resolving. A risk
  is something that might happen. Keeping them apart matters: a risk gets a
  response plan, an issue gets an owner and a due date.

  The decision log underneath is not from the standard. It exists because a
  decision record is only trustworthy if every entry shows where it came from.
source: |
  Issue log: PMBOK Guide, 6th ed., 4.3 Direct and Manage Project Work (output),
  maintained through 4.5 Monitor and Control Project Work.

  Decision log with source states: your own, from your 2026-08-27 rule about
  decisions being attributed without evidence.

field issues
  prompt: What has already gone wrong and still needs resolving?
  check column:owner: An issue with no owner is not being managed. Shared ownership means nobody owns it.
  label: Issues
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: issue
  tone_column: status
  source: Issue log (4.3 output).
  why: |
    Every issue needs one named owner and one date. Shared ownership of an
    issue means nobody owns it.

    The escalation column matters more than it looks: an issue that has been
    open past its due date with no escalation is really a decision to do
    nothing, taken quietly.
  if_blank: |
    Problems live in conversations, get re-explained every week, and the same
    one is solved twice by different people.
  example: |
    I1 | Qt deploy step missed, app will not start from a clean folder | High | Delivery team | 2026-09-16 | Closed
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column issue
    label: Issue
    type: text
    width: 5
  column raised_on
    label: Raised
    type: date
    width: 2
  column priority
    label: Priority
    type: choice
    options: High=danger, Medium=warn, Low=faint
    width: 1
  column owner
    label: Owner
    type: line
    width: 2
  column due
    label: Due
    type: date
    width: 2
  column resolution
    label: Resolution
    type: text
    width: 4
  column status
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Open=danger, In progress=accent, Escalated=warn, Closed=ok
    width: 2

field decisions
  prompt: What has been decided, and how do you know?
  check rows>=3: A project with fewer than three recorded decisions has been making them somewhere else.
  check column:source_state: Every entry needs a source. Specified, agreed, or unobjected: only the first two are decisions.
  label: Decision log
  type: table
  origin: yours
  view: log
  title_column: decision
  tone_column: source_state
  source: |
    Not in the standard. Your rule: a decision is only attributed to you with a
    quote and a date. Three states — specified (you said it), agreed (you
    approved a summary), unobjected (proposed, no reply). Only the first two
    count as decisions.
  why: |
    Projects accumulate "we decided X" claims that nobody can trace. The source
    column makes the difference between a decision and an assumption visible at
    a glance, and the evidence column makes it checkable.

    An unobjected entry is not a decision. It is a proposal that has not been
    answered, and it should either be chased or dropped.
  if_blank: |
    Decisions get attributed to people who never made them, and the record
    stops being worth consulting.
  example: |
    D5 | Hybrid framework: predictive phases, sprints inside Executing | specified | "hybrid sounds like the best option" | 2026-09-15
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column decided_on
    label: Date
    type: date
    width: 2
  column decision
    label: Decision
    type: text
    width: 5
  column source_state
    label: Source
    type: choice
    options: specified=ok, agreed=accent, unobjected=warn
    width: 2
  column evidence
    label: Evidence (quote)
    type: text
    width: 5
  column affects
    label: Affects
    type: line
    width: 2
